# STEP 0.4.1 — sanitizer grounding probe (reproducible evidence for the CPython suppression policy)

Reproduce: podman run --rm -v ./scripts:/probe:ro,Z localhost/motifbo-env:phase0 bash /probe/sanitizer_probe.sh
Build profile: -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -shared -fPIC

Findings (each labelled section maps to SANITIZER_POLICY.md):
  A: CPython leaks ~940KB/840 allocs by design at teardown (PyUnicode/PyImport/_PyEval).
  D: operational detect_leaks=0 -> fully clean.
  C: UBSan silent on the candidate's Cython-generated C (no entries warranted).
  F: module-level 'leak:python3.12' OVER-suppresses genuine candidate leaks.
  H: function-level allocator patterns UNDER-suppress CPython (699B/56 residual).
     => CPython-vs-candidate leak discrimination via LSan suppressions is not robust
        in embedded mode; detect_leaks=0 is the operational choice.
  G: memory-SAFETY gate intact — heap-buffer-overflow CAUGHT, child exits 1,
     independent of leak detection (this is what §3.3 actually requires).

